<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Phu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vat Phou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Phu is a ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> temple complex in Champasak province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, built at the foot of a mountain whose summit resembles a linga. A Shaivite sanctuary from around the 5th century, rebuilt mainly in the 11th and 12th centuries, it is linked to the early Chenla state and lay on a road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>. It is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist site and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phu.jpg" alt="Wat Phu" width="320" height="128" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mattana · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champasak,_Laos,_Wat_Poo_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
